  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>The reading program, in two levels, teaches the sounds, sounding out, reading the fast way, the words that do not follow their sounds, sentences, and stories, and from lesson 40 it times a passage the child reads alone. The math program, in three levels and a bridge into second grade, covers the number, operations, measurement, data, and geometry skills of the Common Core standards for Kindergarten and First Grade, and the mathematics of the Head Start Early Learning Outcomes Framework for Pre-K. Each level has a mastery test after every tenth lesson, and each subject has a placement test to find where a child should begin.</p>
  <p>It is built on Direct Instruction, the method developed by Siegfried Engelmann. Each new step is shown, done together, and then done alone. Errors are corrected the same way every time. Skills run side by side, and each is reviewed at widening intervals until it is permanent.</p>

  <h2>Why it was built</h2>
  <p>Many children struggle with early reading and math not because they are hard but because they were taught unclearly or out of order. Direct Instruction removes the guesswork. Teach Direct gives a parent at home the same careful sequence, scripts, and checks that a well-run classroom program provides.</p>
  <p>It has no games, points, or prizes. The aim is for the child to learn to read and to know the mathematics, and to feel the quiet confidence that comes from knowing them. The child sees only letters, pictures, and numbers on the screen; you read every word.</p>

  <h2>Who made it</h2>
  <p>Teach Direct is built and maintained by <a href="https://www.pikespeakwebdesigns.com">Pikes Peak Web Designs</a>. The source code is on <a href="https://github.com/jonajinga/math-direct">GitHub</a>, under the MIT License. Corrections and suggestions are welcome.</p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
